BUG: registering two route groups with the same prefix, each with its own middleware, makes the first group's routes 404:

```go
e := echo.New()
g1 := e.Group("/group", m1)
g1.GET("", h)
g2 := e.Group("/group", m2)
g2.GET("/other", h)
```

`GET /group` returns 404 even though it is registered; only the second group works. Expected: `GET /group` runs `h` with `m1`; `GET /group/other` runs `h` with `m2`.

ROOT CAUSE: `Group.Use` (group.go) registers catch-all routes `g.Any("", NotFoundHandler)` and `g.Any("/*", NotFoundHandler)` so that group middleware runs for unmatched requests. `Any` registers the NotFoundHandler for every HTTP method, so the second group's `Use` overwrites the first group's real `GET /group` handler.

FIX (this mirrors the upstream design): introduce a special pseudo-method `RouteNotFound` used only as a fallback when no handler exists for the request method.

1) /echo/echo.go — add this exported constant near the other top-level constants (e.g. right before `const (\n\t// Version of Echo`):

```go
// RouteNotFound is special method name used to register a route that is executed when no other route matches
const RouteNotFound = "echo_route_not_found"
```

2) /echo/echo.go — add this method next to the other route-registration methods (e.g. right after `func (e *Echo) Any(...)`):

```go
// RouteNotFound registers a special-case route which is executed when no other route is found (i.e. HTTP 404 cases)
// for current host and method. `Echo#RouteNotFound()` can be used for routes that should be matched only when no
// other route matches.
func (e *Echo) RouteNotFound(path string, h HandlerFunc, m ...MiddlewareFunc) *Route {
	return e.Add(RouteNotFound, path, h, m...)
}
```

3) /echo/router.go — add a `routeNotFound HandlerFunc` field to the `methodHandler` struct; in `func (n *node) addHandler(method string, h HandlerFunc)` add `case RouteNotFound:\n\t\tn.methodHandler.routeNotFound = h`; in `func (n *node) findHandler(method string) HandlerFunc` add `case RouteNotFound:\n\t\treturn n.methodHandler.routeNotFound`. Do NOT add RouteNotFound to `checkMethodNotAllowed` and do NOT add it to the `methods` array in echo.go.

4) /echo/router.go — in `Router.Find`, right after `ctx.handler = currentNode.findHandler(method)` insert:

```go
	if ctx.handler == nil {
		ctx.handler = currentNode.findHandler(RouteNotFound)
	}
```

so the tail of Find reads:

```go
	ctx.handler = currentNode.findHandler(method)
	if ctx.handler == nil {
		ctx.handler = currentNode.findHandler(RouteNotFound)
	}
	ctx.path = currentNode.ppath
	ctx.pnames = currentNode.pnames

	if ctx.handler == nil {
		ctx.handler = currentNode.checkMethodNotAllowed()
	}
	return
```

5) /echo/group.go — in `Group.Use`, replace `g.Any("", NotFoundHandler)` / `g.Any("/*", NotFoundHandler)` with `g.RouteNotFound("", NotFoundHandler)` / `g.RouteNotFound("/*", NotFoundHandler)`, and add:

```go
// RouteNotFound implements `Echo#RouteNotFound()` for sub-routes within the Group.
//
// Example: `g.RouteNotFound("/*", func(c echo.Context) error { return c.NoContent(http.StatusNotFound) })`
func (g *Group) RouteNotFound(path string, h HandlerFunc, m ...MiddlewareFunc) *Route {
	return g.Add(RouteNotFound, path, h, m...)
}
```

